{% comment %}
  sections/quick-add.liquid

  The section the quick add modal renders.

  `assets/dialog.js` requests `{{ product.url }}?section_id=quick-add` and morphs
  the `[data-quick-add-content]` element out of the response into the modal
  body. This file did not exist — so every quick add in the theme fetched a
  section id Shopify could not resolve, every request came back 404, and the
  modal opened onto its own loading paragraph and stayed there.

  Exactly the failure `sections/predictive-search.liquid` was written to fix, in
  exactly the same shape, and the fix is the same: the section the JavaScript
  already asks for, written in Liquid, so money formatting, translations,
  metafields and the swatch settings are the theme's single copies rather than a
  second set reconstructed in JavaScript.

  ## It is not rendered by any template, and that is correct

  A section requested through `?section_id=` does not have to appear on the page.
  Shopify renders it against the requested URL with no layout, which is what
  makes a product URL the right thing to ask. It therefore has no preset and no
  editable settings of its own — a merchant can never select it in the editor, so
  settings here would be a panel nobody can open. Everything configurable lives
  in **Theme settings › Product cards › Quick add modal** instead.

  ## What is in the modal

    Title and description   the product's own
    Options                 `snippets/variant-picker.liquid`, the same one the
                            product page uses, so buttons, dropdowns and swatches
                            all follow the theme's swatch settings
    Purchase options        `<selling-plan-selector>`, one-time against
                            subscribe, when the product has selling plans
    Quantity and add        a real `<form action="/cart/add" method="post">`

  ## `data-product-root` is what makes two of these safe

  `<variant-picker>`, `<product-form>`, `<selling-plan-selector>` and
  `<inventory-status>` all resolve their peers through
  `this.closest('[data-product-root]') || document`. Without that attribute here,
  a modal opened on top of a product page would find the *page's* picker and
  form, and choosing a flavour in the modal would change the variant behind it.

  ## The add button's label is written by JavaScript

  `product-form.js` rewrites it on every variant change — "Add to cart",
  "Pre-order", "Sold out" — and `<quick-add-summary>` then appends the price and
  the chosen option to whatever the form wrote. See `assets/quick-add.js`.

  Both used to set `button.textContent`, which replaces every child the button
  has. So the button could hold text and nothing else, and the arrow badge the
  drawing puts on its end is drawn by `::after` in `assets/base.css` because a
  pseudo-element is not a child and `textContent` cannot reach it.

  That stopped being enough the moment the button needed a spinner: an arrow is
  decoration and can live in a pseudo-element, but a loader that replaces the
  label has to be a sibling of it. So the words are in a `[data-button-label]`
  span and both writers aim at that instead — `labelTarget()` in
  `assets/utilities.js` is the one line of it, and it falls back to the button
  for every other add button in the theme.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  comment
    `product` is the global for the URL this section was requested against, which
    is what makes `{{ product.url }}?section_id=quick-add` the whole of the
    request. Nothing is passed in and nothing needs to be.
  endcomment
  assign current = product.selected_or_first_available_variant

  comment
    The modal is one product wide, so the form id only has to be unique against
    the page underneath it — which is a product page often enough to matter. The
    product id is what distinguishes them.
  endcomment
  assign form_id = 'quick-add-' | append: product.id | append: '-form'
  assign qty_id = 'QuickAddQty-' | append: product.id

  assign show_description = settings.quick_add_show_description
  assign show_plans = settings.quick_add_show_selling_plans
  assign has_plans = false
  if show_plans and product.selling_plan_groups.size > 0
    assign has_plans = true
  endif

  comment
    The benefit list under Subscribe & save is one line of the textarea per
    benefit. `newline_to_br` then `split` rather than `split: '\n'`, because a
    Liquid string literal has no escape sequences and "\n" there is a backslash
    followed by an n.
  endcomment
  assign benefits = settings.quick_add_benefits | newline_to_br | split: '<br />'

  assign first_allocation = current.selling_plan_allocations.first
-%}

<div
  class="quick-add__content"
  data-quick-add-content
  data-product-root
  data-section-id="quick-add"
  data-product-id="{{ product.id }}"
>
  {%- comment -%}
    The whole product, as JSON, for anything in the modal that needs a price or
    a title without another request. `<quick-add-summary>` reads it to build the
    add button's label; `<product-bundle>` reads it to fill a bundle slot from a
    variant the customer chose here.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script type="application/json" data-quick-add-product>
    {
      "id": {{ product.id }},
      "title": {{ product.title | json }},
      "handle": {{ product.handle | json }},
      "url": {{ product.url | json }},
      "featured_image": {{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 200 | json }},
      "has_only_default_variant": {{ product.has_only_default_variant | json }},
      "variants": {{ product.variants | json }}
    }
  </script>

  <header class="quick-add__heading">
    <h2 class="quick-add__title">
      <a href="{{ product.url }}">{{ product.title | escape }}</a>
    </h2>

    {%- if show_description and product.description != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        One line, not the whole description. The modal exists so a customer does
        not have to leave the grid; a full product description in it is the
        product page, badly.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <p class="quick-add__summary">
        {{- product.description | strip_html | truncatewords: 26 -}}
      </p>
    {%- endif -%}
  </header>

  {%- comment -%}
    The dashed rule under the heading. A `<hr>` rather than a border, because it
    separates the identity of the product from the choices being made about it,
    and that is a thematic break.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <hr class="quick-add__rule">

  <div class="quick-add__options">
    {%- unless product.has_only_default_variant -%}
      {% render 'variant-picker',
         product: product,
         section: section,
         update_url: false,
         form_id: form_id %}
    {%- endunless -%}
  </div>

  {%- if has_plans -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The purchase options, as two cards rather than the plain radio pair
      `snippets/selling-plans.liquid` renders.

      Every ref `assets/product-selling-plans.js` requires is still here and
      still spelled the same — `oneTime`, `subscribe`, `interval`, `summary`,
      `unavailable`, and `[data-allocations]`. The component is untouched; only
      what sits around its inputs is different, which is why this can be richer
      without a second component to keep in step.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <selling-plan-selector
      class="quick-add__plans selling-plans selling-plans--cards"
      data-layout="cards"
    >
      <script type="application/json" data-allocations>
        {
          {%- for v in product.variants -%}
            "{{ v.id }}": [
              {%- for allocation in v.selling_plan_allocations -%}
                {
                  "id": {{ allocation.selling_plan.id }},
                  "name": {{ allocation.selling_plan.name | json }},
                  "price": {{ allocation.price }},
                  "compare_at_price": {{ allocation.compare_at_price | default: v.price }},
                  "per_delivery_price": {{ allocation.per_delivery_price }}
                }{%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            ]{%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
        }
      </script>

      <fieldset class="selling-plans__options">
        <legend class="visually-hidden">{{ 'products.selling_plans.heading' | t }}</legend>

        <label class="selling-plans__card" data-purchase-option>
          <input type="radio" name="purchase-option" value="" data-ref="oneTime" checked>

          <span class="selling-plans__card-main">
            <span class="selling-plans__card-title">
              {{ 'products.selling_plans.one_time' | t }}
            </span>

            {%- if settings.quick_add_one_time_note != blank -%}
              <span class="selling-plans__card-note">
                {{ settings.quick_add_one_time_note }}
              </span>
            {%- endif -%}
          </span>

          <span class="selling-plans__card-price" data-one-time-price>
            {% render 'price', variant: current, context: 'product', show_badges: true %}
          </span>
        </label>

        <label class="selling-plans__card" data-purchase-option>
          <input type="radio" name="purchase-option" value="subscription" data-ref="subscribe">

          <span class="selling-plans__card-main">
            <span class="selling-plans__card-title">
              {{ 'products.selling_plans.subscription' | t }}

              {%- if settings.quick_add_recommended_badge != blank -%}
                <span class="selling-plans__card-badge">
                  {{- settings.quick_add_recommended_badge -}}
                </span>
              {%- endif -%}
            </span>

            {%- comment -%}
              The frequency select sits inside the card it belongs to. The
              component hides and disables it while One-time is chosen, so it is
              never a control a customer can set and have ignored.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <label class="visually-hidden" for="QuickAddPlan-{{ product.id }}">
              {{ 'products.selling_plans.select_plan' | t }}
            </label>
            <select
              id="QuickAddPlan-{{ product.id }}"
              class="selling-plans__interval"
              data-ref="interval"
              name="selling_plan"
              form="{{ form_id }}"
              hidden
              disabled
            ></select>

            {%- if benefits.size > 0 -%}
              <span class="selling-plans__benefits">
                {%- for benefit in benefits -%}
                  {%- assign line = benefit | strip_html | strip -%}
                  {%- if line != blank -%}
                    <span class="selling-plans__benefit">
                      {% render 'icon', icon: 'check', size: 12 %}
                      {{ line }}
                    </span>
                  {%- endif -%}
                {%- endfor -%}
              </span>
            {%- endif -%}
          </span>

          <span class="selling-plans__card-price" data-plan-price>
            {%- if first_allocation -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                Written out rather than rendered through `snippets/price.liquid`,
                because a selling plan allocation is not a variant and the
                snippet prices variants. The class names and the
                `data-price-*` hooks are the snippet's, so it styles the same
                and `<quick-add-summary>` finds it the same way.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- liquid
                assign plan_saved = first_allocation.compare_at_price | minus: first_allocation.price
                assign plan_percent = 0
                if first_allocation.compare_at_price > 0
                  assign plan_percent = plan_saved | times: 100.0 | divided_by: first_allocation.compare_at_price | round
                endif
              -%}
              <span class="price price--subscription">
                <span class="price__regular" data-price-current>{{ first_allocation.price | money }}</span>
                <s
                  class="price__compare"
                  data-price-compare
                  {% unless first_allocation.compare_at_price > first_allocation.price %}hidden{% endunless %}
                >
                  {{- first_allocation.compare_at_price | money -}}
                </s>

                {%- comment -%}
                  The saving, said the same way the one-time card says it.

                  This card had no badge at all, so the drawing's pair — "Save
                  10%" under the one-time price and "Save 20%" under the
                  subscription — only ever showed one of them, and the discount
                  that is the whole reason to subscribe was the one missing.

                  `data-price-saving` and the same string as `price.liquid`, so
                  `assets/quick-add.js` keeps both in step on a variant change
                  and the two cards cannot word the same thing differently.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                <span
                  class="price__saving"
                  data-price-saving
                  {% unless plan_percent > 0 %}hidden{% endunless %}
                >
                  {{- 'products.product.sale_percent' | t: percent: plan_percent -}}
                </span>
              </span>
            {%- endif -%}
          </span>
        </label>
      </fieldset>

      <p class="selling-plans__summary" data-ref="summary" role="status"></p>
      <p class="selling-plans__unavailable" data-ref="unavailable" hidden></p>
    </selling-plan-selector>
  {%- else -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      No subscriptions on this product, so the price is its own row rather than
      a column of a card that is not there.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="quick-add__price">
      {% render 'price', variant: current, context: 'product', show_badges: true %}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  <product-form class="quick-add__buy buy-buttons" data-product-id="{{ product.id }}">
    <form
      action="{{ routes.cart_add_url }}"
      method="post"
      id="{{ form_id }}"
      class="quick-add__form"
      data-ref="form"
      novalidate="novalidate"
    >
      <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ current.id }}" data-ref="idInput">

      {% render 'quantity-selector',
         id: qty_id,
         min: 1,
         step: 1,
         product_title: product.title %}

      {%- comment -%}
        The words go in a span, and the spinner is its sibling — see the note at
        the top on why they could not both simply be children of the button.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <button
        type="submit"
        class="button button--primary quick-add__submit"
        data-ref="submit"
        {% unless current.available %}disabled{% endunless %}
      >
        <span data-button-label>
          {%- if current.available -%}
            {{ 'products.product.add_to_cart' | t }}
          {%- else -%}
            {{ 'products.product.sold_out' | t }}
          {%- endif -%}
        </span>

        {%- comment -%}
          Shown while `<product-form>` has the request in flight, in place of the
          label. Adding from the modal is a network round trip and the modal
          closes on success, so until this the only sign a press had landed was
          the modal eventually going away.

          It is the same spinner the tray's button and every bundle row use, so
          "working" looks like one thing across the section.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <span class="quick-add__submit-loader">{% render 'icon', icon: 'loader', size: 18 %}</span>
      </button>

      <p class="quick-add__error" data-ref="error" role="alert" hidden></p>
    </form>
  </product-form>

  {%- comment -%}
    Decorates the button's label with the price and the chosen option, after
    `product-form.js` has written it, and reports which option is selected up
    beside the picker's heading. Renders nothing on its own — it only ever
    rewrites text that is already there.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <quick-add-summary
    class="visually-hidden"
    data-product-id="{{ product.id }}"
    data-add-label="{{ 'products.product.add_to_cart' | t | escape }}"
  ></quick-add-summary>

  {%- if settings.quick_add_show_details_link or settings.quick_add_footnote != blank -%}
    <footer class="quick-add__footer">
      {%- if settings.quick_add_show_details_link -%}
        <a class="quick-add__details" href="{{ product.url }}">
          {{ 'products.product.view_details' | t | default: product.title }}
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if settings.quick_add_footnote != blank -%}
        <span class="quick-add__footnote">{{ settings.quick_add_footnote }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </footer>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:sections.quick_add.name",
  "settings": []
}
{% endschema %}
